A||||4||doughboySE||02 senser hard to remove and replace?||||1||
Z||000000||doughboySE||05-29-2000||10:57 PM||||well? thats the question man my car is really losing power bad its like its really struggling and sucking gas bad . codes point to the senser want to do it myself if i can <BR>what you guys think?||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||05-29-2000||11:18 PM||||Dough, it's pretty easy.  I found I could make more room by taking off the intake to the Holley.  Hardest thing to do is to get at that damn plug!  It's back behind the engine, so you better let the exhaust cool a lot before reaching in and trying to unplug it.  <BR>I used a plug wrench to remove the sensor, and she was very tight.  I had to run the engine for a minite or so to heat it up and then it broke loose...use lots of liquid wrench while she is cold.<BR>Also, you will have to cut off the wire on the old sensor to get the wrench on. <BR>I used a combination wrench to tighten up the new sensor.  Clean up the threads as best you can....more liquid wrench...and be sure you have the sensor threads started (not crossed) by hand before tightening it.<P>This all assumes the exhaust pipes are in good order!<BR>Or you could ask a friend to help, lol, Dave  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||BroCar 87||05-30-2000||02:33 PM||||I must agree with DAVED, "If all pipes are in good order"... When I did my O2 sensor, my pipes were nasty, rusted, and cracked. I barely got the wrench on there and the threads broke out of the manifold. I had to take my exhaust off and go get a new manifold from the junkyard.<BR>NOT FUN  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tiacPaul||05-30-2000||11:36 PM||||is this the four or six cylinder we're talking about?  The o2 on my four took about 5 min!  I didn't have to cut any wires either.  Every sensor has a plug somewhere.  I just unpluged it and unscrewed it with a normal wrench.  Do watch the exaust though and me sure the new sensor has antiseize compound on it before putting it in or it will never come back out!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phaeton||05-31-2000||03:49 AM||||Auto parts stores have special sockets available with cutout sides for the wire, costs less than an O2 sensor and lasts forever.  OK, it just lasts until lost, but it still makes the job more pleasant.<P>tiacPaul speaks truth about the antiseize!!||||reg||12
